---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';

const people = (await getCollection('family')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const rendered = await Promise.all(
  people.map(async (person) => {
    // A person may have a single `photo` or a `photos` list (young + old portraits).
    const portraits = person.data.photos?.length
      ? person.data.photos.map((p) => ({ src: p.src, caption: p.caption }))
      : person.data.photo
        ? [{ src: person.data.photo }]
        : [];
    return { person, portraits, Content: (await render(person)).Content };
  }),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Family"
  description="The Desha and Searcy families — from a Kentucky governor to the builders of the Franklin Desha House in Independence County, Arkansas."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">The people</span>
    <h1>The Desha &amp; Searcy Families</h1>
    <p>
      Two families, joined by marriage, whose names are woven into the history of Arkansas — a Kentucky
      governor, county namesakes, a territorial judge, and the people who built this house.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        Franklin Desha built this house in 1847 on land his family had worked for years. He was the
        grandson of a Kentucky governor, the son of a Robert Desha who settled at Helena, and the nephew
        of the Benjamin Desha for whom Desha County is named. He married <strong>Elizabeth Searcy</strong>,
        daughter of <strong>Richard Searcy</strong> — the first clerk and judge in the Arkansas Territory,
        for whom both the city of Searcy and Searcy County are named.
      </p>
      <p>
        Their house passed down through their descendants — through the Stroud and Vandiver lines — and
        remains a family home today.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  {
    rendered.map(({ person, portraits, Content }) => (
      <section class="section">
        <span class="eyebrow">{person.data.relationship ?? 'Family'}</span>
        <h2>{person.data.name}</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          {person.data.born && <>Born {person.data.born}.</>}
          {person.data.died && <> Died {person.data.died}.</>}
        </p>
        {portraits.length > 0 && (
          <figure class="portrait-gallery">
            {portraits.map((p) => (
              <figure class="portrait">
                <img src={p.src} alt={`Portrait of ${person.data.name}`} loading="lazy" />
                {p.caption && <figcaption>{p.caption}</figcaption>}
              </figure>
            ))}
          </figure>
        )}
        <article class="prose">
          <Content />
        </article>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="section">
    <h2>A note on the family tree</h2>
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        An earlier note here flagged a seeming contradiction: the published record lists a Robert
        Desha (1791–1849) who was a Tennessee congressman and the brother of Governor Joseph Desha,
        but who moved to Mobile, Alabama, not Helena. Yet the Franklin Desha House's National
        Register record names Franklin's father as a Robert Desha who settled Helena, Arkansas.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a contradiction — they were two different men, in two different generations.
        The Robert Desha who moved to Mobile was Governor Desha's <strong>brother</strong>;
        Franklin's father, the Robert Desha who settled Helena, was Governor Desha's
        <strong>son</strong>. That is why Franklin is the governor's grandson. The family's own
        records — Bibles, deeds, probate papers, and census records — remain the final authority,
        and a full family tree with source citations is coming soon to this site.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
